from ../LanguageData import ConditionMarkers, ContainerProperties
from ./Facts import CapabilityForm, ChoiceForm
from ../model/OpenItems import Ambiguity

export abstract anchor ConditionKind as condition-kind:
    description:: string
    notation:: string

export abstract anchor Condition:
    id:: string
    kind:: ConditionKind
    subject:: string
    aspect:: null:: string
    value:: any
    positive:: boolean

export anchor Conditions:
    description:
        Many requirements only hold some of the time. When disabled, the
        button cannot be clicked does not say the button can never be
        clicked; it says so only while the button is disabled. A condition is
        the part of a fact that says when it holds, and analysis keeps it
        separate from the rest of the fact rather than folding it into the
        wording.

        A fact can have any number of conditions, and holds only when all of
        them do. A fact with no conditions holds always.

    kinds:
        There are two kinds of condition. A state condition is about how
        something is: when the button is disabled. An event condition is
        about something happening: when the button is clicked. They are
        listed in CONDITION_KINDS below.

    waysToWriteOne:
        A condition can come before the statement it limits, after it,
        attached to the noun it is about, or from the structure around it.

        ```text
        When disabled, the button cannot be clicked.       before
        The button cannot be clicked when disabled.        after
        The disabled button cannot be clicked.             attached to the noun
        ```

        ```piton
        export anchor Button:
            disabled:
                It cannot be clicked.                       from structure
        ```

        The first two are found by the words in @{ConditionMarkers}. Unless
        opens a condition that is negated: unless enabled means when not
        enabled.

        The third is found by the same test as a condition from structure,
        below: a word describing the subject, here disabled, becomes a
        condition only when it is one of the values of a choice fact about
        the subject. Otherwise it is an ordinary description, and the
        statement is read without a condition.

    conditionsFromStructure:
        A property can stand for a condition. In the Button above, disabled
        is not a part of the button; it is a state the button can be in, and
        everything written inside it holds in that state.

        Analysis only reads a property this way when it can prove it. The
        property's name must be one of the values of a choice fact about the
        property's owner, such as Button.state is one of enabled, disabled.
        Then every fact read inside the property gets the condition
        owner.aspect = name. When the property's name is not such a value,
        the property is only a topic: the facts inside it are about the owner
        and carry no condition.

        Properties named in @{ContainerProperties}, such as description, are
        never conditions. They hold prose about their owner.

    stateOrEvent:
        A condition written with a past participle can mean either kind. When
        disabled is a state; when clicked is an event. Analysis decides using
        the facts it already has about the subject.

        ```text
        the word is a value of a choice about the subject     state condition
        the word's verb is a capability of the subject        event condition
        both                                                  ambiguity
        neither                                               unresolved
        ```

        The first test uses facts of the @{ChoiceForm}, and the second facts of
        the @{CapabilityForm}. For Button, disabled is one of the values in
        Button.state is one of enabled, disabled, so when disabled is a state
        condition. Clicked is the past form of click, and Button can be clicked,
        so when clicked is an event condition. When both readings are possible,
        analysis records an @{Ambiguity} with both, and uses neither. When
        neither is, the fact keeps the condition as an unresolved part and
        becomes partial.

    missingSubjects:
        A condition often leaves out its subject, as in when disabled. The
        subject is then the subject of the statement the condition limits.

    exclusion:
        Two conditions exclude each other when they require different values
        of the same aspect, and that aspect has a choice fact. When
        Button.state = enabled and when Button.state = disabled exclude each
        other, because the button has one state at a time. Conditions that do
        not exclude each other are said to overlap. Whether two conditions
        overlap decides whether two facts can contradict each other.

    conditionKinds: {CONDITION_KINDS}

export condition-kind StateCondition:
    notation: when subject.aspect = value
    description:
        Holds while something has a given value, typically one of the values
        of a choice fact.

export condition-kind EventCondition:
    notation: on subject action
    description:
        Holds at the moment an action happens to something, typically an
        action named by a capability fact.

export CONDITION_KINDS:: extends ConditionKind[]:
    - {StateCondition}
    - {EventCondition}
